integral-form-4ae26d18.conceptual_definition:
The Biot-Savart Law in integral form quantifies the magnetic field (
\mathbf{B}
) generated at a specific point in space (
\mathbf{r}
) due to a steady electric current (
I
) flowing through a wire. It states that the magnetic field is the sum (integral) of infinitesimal contributions from each segment of the current-carrying wire, where each contribution is proportional to the current, inversely proportional to the square of the distance from the segment to the observation point, and dependent on the orientation of the current segment relative to the observation point.

L.description:
The Lagrangian of a physical system, often defined as the difference between its kinetic energy and potential energy (
L = K - V
). It is a function of the system's generalized coordinates, generalized velocities, and potentially time.
